**New Evidence**
- Marcos-linked health rollout: DOH launched `PinasLakas` on July 26, 2022 as part of the president's first 100 days, targeting 90 percent of senior citizens and 50 percent booster coverage for the eligible population.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PinasLakas vaccination launch","publisher":"Department of Health","publication_date":"2022-07-27","url":"https://ncroffice.doh.gov.ph/LatestNews/Details/378","claim":"The DOH launched PinasLakas on July 26, 2022 to intensify COVID-19 vaccination in Marcos's first 100 days, targeting 90 percent of senior citizens and 50 percent booster coverage for eligible Filipinos.","locator":"news release excerpt in search result","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct official report of a dated public-health campaign with explicit targets and presidential linkage.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct administration-level attribution; the campaign was implemented by DOH under the Marcos administration.","contrary_evidence":["This is a rollout target, not proof of final health outcomes.","Operational delivery still depended on DOH and local partners."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.6","6B.10"]}
- Follow-through on vaccination: DOH later reported that by October 6, 2022 the campaign had boosted millions of people and lifted full vaccination to 93.81 percent, with first-booster coverage at 25.62 percent.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DOH achieves major strides in fight vs COVID-19, strengthens PH healthcare system","publisher":"Department of Health","publication_date":"2022-10-06","url":"https://caro.doh.gov.ph/doh-achieves-major-strides-in-fight-vs-covid-19-strengthens-ph-healthcare-system/","claim":"The DOH said PinasLakas raised full vaccination to 93.81 percent and first-booster coverage to 25.62 percent by October 6, 2022, including 3,433,713 boosted eligible individuals and 45,255 boosted senior citizens.","locator":"campaign results paragraph in search snippet","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government report with concrete coverage figures and a dated checkpoint.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but meaningful: this was a Marcos-directed public-health push executed through DOH.","contrary_evidence":["The gains occurred in an inherited pandemic-response system.","The article is an institutional progress report, not an independent evaluation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.6","6B.10"]}
- Social protection delivery was simplified: on August 3, 2022 DSWD said it had issued updated AICS guidelines to expedite aid, streamline intake and eligibility forms, and increase beneficiaries served.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Request for assistance under AICS now easier for clients","publisher":"Department of Social Welfare and Development","publication_date":"2022-08-03","url":"https://dswd.gov.ph/request-for-assistance-under-aics-now-easier-for-clients-dswd/","claim":"DSWD said updated AICS guidelines expedited aid by simplifying intake and eligibility forms, shortening procedures, and increasing beneficiaries served.","locator":"paragraphs 110-121","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct 2022 agency announcement with specific procedural changes and implementation effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Administration-level attribution is partial; the reform was implemented by DSWD under Secretary Tulfo, within the Marcos administration.","contrary_evidence":["This is an implementation reform, not a measured welfare outcome.","The article attributes the directive to the secretary rather than Marcos personally."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.7","6B.10"]}
- DSWD's crisis-mental-health response was institutionalized in 2022: WiSUPPORT became a regular program and flagship mental-health initiative for children, PWDs, seniors, OFWs in distress, women in difficult circumstances, first responders, and others.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DSWD, partners call for stronger mental health initiatives in 30th National Children’s Month Celebration","publisher":"Department of Social Welfare and Development","publication_date":"2022-11-03","url":"https://old.dswd.gov.ph/dswd-partners-call-for-stronger-mental-health-initiatives-in-30th-national-childrens-month-celebration/","claim":"DSWD said Memorandum Circular No. 19 of 2022 institutionalized WiSUPPORT as a regular program and flagship mental-health intervention for children, PWDs, seniors, OFWs in distress, women in difficult circumstances, first responders, caregivers, and others in distress.","locator":"lines 127-130","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Dated agency announcement with a precise program definition and target groups.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Administration-level attribution; the program sits within the Marcos-era DSWD, not a personal presidential directive.","contrary_evidence":["Program design does not prove reach or quality in all cases.","The page focuses on advocacy, not audited outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.4","6B.6","6B.9"]}
- Education recovery was explicitly planned in March 2022: DepEd said it was developing a learning recovery framework to address pandemic learning gaps with remediation, learning-support centers, summer interventions, and child-protection and health-support measures.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DepEd develops learning recovery plan as more schools conduct face-to-face classes","publisher":"Department of Education","publication_date":"2022-03-29","url":"https://www.deped.gov.ph/2022/03/29/deped-develops-learning-recovery-plan-as-more-schools-conduct-face-to-face-classes/","claim":"DepEd said it was crafting a learning recovery plan to address pandemic learning gaps through remediation, learning-support centers, reading interventions, home visits, teacher support, and child-protection and health measures.","locator":"lines 280-287","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct, dated policy statement from the education department with concrete implementation design.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect administration-level attribution; the plan was led by DepEd before and during the Marcos transition, with implementation under the new administration.","contrary_evidence":["The plan was crafted before Marcos took office on June 30, 2022.","This is a framework, not proof of improved outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.8","6B.10"]}
- School reopening and ALS were formalized in July 2022: DepEd required the gradual return to five-day in-person classes and said ALS would begin with the opening date, guided by BEDP 2030 and the learning-recovery continuity plan.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DepEd releases guidelines for School Year 2022-2023","publisher":"Department of Education","publication_date":"2022-07-12","url":"https://www.deped.gov.ph/2022/07/12/deped-releases-guidelines-for-school-year-2022-2023/","claim":"DepEd issued School Year 2022-2023 guidelines committing to the resumption of five-day in-person classes, with ALS beginning at school opening and BEDP 2030 and the Learning Recovery and Continuity Plan guiding implementation.","locator":"lines 280-292","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Dated official policy with explicit school-opening and ALS provisions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Administration-level attribution is partial; DepEd led the measure under VP-Secretary Sara Duterte within the Marcos administration.","contrary_evidence":["The policy was executed by DepEd rather than Marcos personally.","It reflects recovery from pandemic disruption more than a new social-welfare architecture."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.8","6B.10"]}
- UNICEF welcomed the full return to school in August 2022 and said vulnerable children, including children with disabilities and those in disaster and conflict zones, were lagging furthest; it also cited support for WASH and rapid literacy assessment.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNICEF celebrates learners’ first day back in school","publisher":"UNICEF Philippines","publication_date":"2022-08-22","url":"https://www.unicef.org/philippines/press-releases/unicef-celebrates-learners-first-day-back-school","claim":"UNICEF said millions of children returned to school on August 22, 2022, warned that learning poverty hit vulnerable children with disabilities and those in disaster or conflict zones hardest, and noted support for WASH and rapid literacy assessments.","locator":"lines 59-70","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct UNICEF press release with precise date, policy context, and vulnerable-group framing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; this documents the education environment and partner-supported recovery under the Marcos administration rather than a single presidential order.","contrary_evidence":["UNICEF is describing system recovery, not assigning credit to Marcos personally.","The learning crisis predated the administration."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.5","6B.8","6B.10"]}
- ALS recovery for marginalized learners advanced in June 2022: UNICEF and DepEd launched the ALS 2.0 ICT Strategic Plan and Research Agenda to support out-of-school youth and adults through a more flexible second-chance education path.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DepEd, UNICEF strengthen Alternative Learning System toward quality, relevant second chance basic education","publisher":"UNICEF Philippines","publication_date":"2022-06-09","url":"https://www.unicef.org/philippines/press-releases/deped-unicef-strengthen-alternative-learning-system-toward-quality-relevant-second","claim":"UNICEF and DepEd launched the ALS 2.0 ICT Strategic Plan and Research Agenda to help out-of-school youth and adults access flexible pathways to complete basic education and improve their lives.","locator":"lines 63-74","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct joint press release with explicit reform content and target population.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect administration-level attribution; DepEd led the policy, and it fits Marcos-era education recovery.","contrary_evidence":["The measure was led by DepEd and UNICEF, not directly by Marcos.","It is a program-strengthening step, not a proven outcome measure."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.8","6B.9"]}
- UNICEF's October 2022 bulletin said its Philippines work from April to September 2022 included accelerating learning recovery, bringing child protection services closer via TeleCPUs, and building a more equitable world through digital public goods.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"For every child, results - Issue 3","publisher":"UNICEF Philippines","publication_date":"2022-10","url":"https://www.unicef.org/philippines/reports/every-child-results-issue-3","claim":"UNICEF said its Philippines work from April to September 2022 included accelerating learning recovery, bringing child protection services closer through TeleCPUs, and building a more equitable world through digital public goods.","locator":"lines 65-71","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Bulletin-style report with a dated coverage window and concrete program themes, though not a ruler-specific audit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"April to September 2022 coverage within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; useful as evidence of the welfare service environment under the Marcos administration, not of direct presidential authorship.","contrary_evidence":["This is a UNICEF summary, not a Marcos performance evaluation.","It aggregates partner work across multiple actors."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.8","6B.9"]}
- Nutrition and disaster response remained active in 2022: UNICEF said Southern Leyte health workers were better able to address malnutrition after support, with 160 SAM and 565 MAM cases recorded in the first quarter.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"For every child, nutrition even in emergency situations","publisher":"UNICEF Philippines","publication_date":"2022-07-25","url":"https://www.unicef.org/philippines/stories/every-child-nutrition-even-emergency-situations","claim":"UNICEF said health workers in Southern Leyte were better able to address malnutrition after support, and the province had recorded 160 severe acute malnutrition cases and 565 moderate acute malnutrition cases in the first quarter.","locator":"lines 56-66","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct story with named local figures and a defined disaster-recovery context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; this is crisis-response context rather than a Marcos-issued directive, but it reflects the social need the administration faced.","contrary_evidence":["The article centers on UNICEF and local health workers, not Marcos.","It shows continuing need as much as program response."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
- Vulnerable-child protection was tested in disaster conditions: in Southern Leyte, UNICEF described a child with cerebral palsy whose family's medical follow-up and nutrition were disrupted by Typhoon Odette and road damage.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Supporting Most Vulnerable Children to Fight Malnutrition in Southern Leyte","publisher":"UNICEF Philippines","publication_date":"2022-07-22","url":"https://www.unicef.org/philippines/stories/supporting-most-vulnerable-children-fight-malnutrition-southern-leyte","claim":"UNICEF described a child with cerebral palsy in Southern Leyte whose care and nutrition were disrupted by Typhoon Odette, damaged housing, and blocked roads, illustrating how disasters worsened access for vulnerable families.","locator":"lines 67-78","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct case-based reporting with a clear vulnerability and disaster-access narrative.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect crisis context; not a direct Marcos action, but relevant to the vulnerability baseline his administration inherited and faced.","contrary_evidence":["This is a humanitarian case study, not a ruler-attribution finding.","The article does not test government program effectiveness directly."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
- Disaster shelter policy was tightened under Marcos's first SONA: DHSUD said ESAP would be improved with better coordination with DSWD, giving calamity-affected families emergency cash assistance or construction materials to repair damaged homes.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DHSUD one with Marcos admin in boosting disaster response ops","publisher":"Philippine News Agency","publication_date":"2022-07-26","url":"https://www.pna.gov.ph/articles/1179779","claim":"DHSUD said ESAP would be improved under Marcos directives with better coordination with DSWD, providing calamity-affected families emergency cash assistance or construction materials to repair damaged houses.","locator":"lines 65-80","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Government newswire reporting a specific presidential directive and program change, with direct policy detail.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct administration-level attribution.","contrary_evidence":["The delivery mechanism remained DHSUD/DSWD-led rather than personally presidential.","This is shelter relief, not a full welfare-system evaluation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- The administration also leaned on the national ID and targeted subsidies to protect vulnerable households: NEDA's first-100-days report said the ID system would strengthen monitoring for social-protection distribution, while fuel subsidies were being used for drivers, farmers, and fisherfolk.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Marcos admin ensures continued economic recovery from pandemic","publisher":"Philippine News Agency","publication_date":"2022-10-10","url":"https://www.pna.gov.ph/articles/1185720","claim":"NEDA said the national ID system would strengthen monitoring for social protection distribution, and the government was using fuel subsidies for drivers, farmers, and fisherfolk to cushion price shocks.","locator":"lines 85-105","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official-government newswire with a detailed, dated report on administration measures and implementation path.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct administration-level attribution, though several measures were inherited or jointly managed across agencies.","contrary_evidence":["The report frames measures as recovery management, not durable welfare transformation.","Some components, such as the national ID, were already in motion before Marcos."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.7","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- Food security was a declared social priority: in his first SONA, Marcos said agriculture would drive growth and employment, and the administration would raise production to counter food-price spikes and support consumer purchasing power.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Marcos relays food production agenda in 1st SONA","publisher":"Department of Agriculture","publication_date":"2022-07-26","url":"https://www.da.gov.ph/marcos-relays-food-production-agenda-in-1st-sona/","claim":"Marcos said agriculture would be one of the main drivers of growth and employment and that the administration would raise production to counter food-price spikes and support purchasing power.","locator":"lines 124-143","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential statement published by the implementing department with concrete policy direction.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct.","contrary_evidence":["The claim is a policy direction, not a delivery outcome.","Food-price pressure was partly inherited and global."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across DOH, DepEd, DSWD, DHSUD, DA, PNA, UNICEF, and World Bank/IMF-linked material. I retained 14 distinct URLs above.

- Duplicate removals: I dropped repeated school-reopening and learning-recovery stories that restated the same DepEd or UNICEF message without adding a new mechanism or target group.
- Out-of-period removals: I excluded 2023-2024 follow-ups unless they explicitly described a 2022 act or 2022 implementation step.
- Attribution removals: I rejected several 2022 aid items that were clearly Duterte-era baseline or pre-Marcos programs and did not cleanly support Marcos-period attribution.
- Weak/too-generic removals: I skipped broad landing pages and general development summaries that did not specify a ruler-period action, implementation change, or vulnerable-group effect.
- Blocked/fragile removals: A few official pages were slow or unstable, so I relied on accessible official mirrors, press releases, and search-result excerpts only when they carried a distinct 2022 claim.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct outcome evidence is still thin. I found clear 2022 policy moves on health, education, crisis aid, and food security, but less hard evidence on measurable improvement in school outcomes, malnutrition, poverty, or service quality by end-2022.
- Attribution is often administration-level rather than personal. DepEd, DSWD, and DOH actions were led by agency heads under Marcos, so the dossier should treat Marcos as the period ruler rather than the sole operational actor.
- Distribution evidence is better on vulnerable groups and crisis access than on regional or class-level household effects. More PSA/household-survey breakdowns would help.
- Several items are reform or recovery plans rather than completed delivery. Additional budget-execution, audit, or program-admin data would improve confidence on implementation versus announcement.
